c-04.theory:
# Making decisions

Until now, every program ran all of its lines, always in the same order. With `if`, the program **chooses** what to do according to the data it read (previous lesson).

```c
if (nota >= 50) {
    printf("Aprovado\n");
} else {
    printf("Reprovado\n");
}
```

## True and false in C

C originally had no boolean type. The rule is simple: **zero is false, any other value is true**. Comparisons return `1` (true) or `0` (false):

- `==` equal, `!=` different
- `<`, `<=`, `>`, `>=`

## The most dangerous mistake: = versus ==

`=` **assigns**, `==` **compares**. Look:

```c
if (x = 5) {   /* WRONG: stores 5 in x, and 5 is "true" */
    ...
}
```

That `if` is always true and also ruins the value of `x`. Many compilers only give a warning, so pay attention.

## Several alternatives: else if

```c
if (nota >= 90) {
    printf("A\n");
} else if (nota >= 70) {
    printf("B\n");
} else if (nota >= 50) {
    printf("C\n");
} else {
    printf("D\n");
}
```

The conditions are tested **from top to bottom** and only the first true block runs. That is why order matters: if the `nota >= 50` test came first, a grade of 95 would get "C".

## Logical operators

- `&&` (AND): true only if both sides are true
- `||` (OR): true if at least one side is true
- `!` (NOT): inverts

```c
if (idade >= 18 && idade < 65) { ... }
if (dia == 6 || dia == 7) { ... }
```

C uses **short-circuit evaluation**: in `a && b`, if `a` is false, `b` is not even evaluated. This lets you write `if (n != 0 && 10 / n > 2)` without any risk of dividing by zero.

## Divisibility with %

The remainder of a division is the classic way to test whether one number divides another: `n % 2 == 0` means "n is even"; `ano % 4 == 0` means "ano is a multiple of 4".

## Leap year

The Gregorian calendar defines: a year is a **leap year** if it is divisible by 4, **except** those divisible by 100, which are leap years only if they are also divisible by 400. So 2024 is a leap year, 1900 is not, and 2000 is. Translating a rule like this into `&&` and `||` is exactly the kind of reasoning programming demands: read calmly and use parentheses to make the intent clear.

## A tip about braces

If an `if` has a single statement, the braces are optional. Even so, use them: adding a second line later and forgetting the braces is a classic bug.

c-05.theory:
# Loops

With `if` the program chooses a path. With **loops**, it repeats a block several times. That is what lets you process 10 or 10 thousand numbers with the same code.

## while: repeats while the condition is true

```c
int i = 1;
while (i <= 5) {
    printf("%d\n", i);
    i++;
}
```

`i++` adds 1 to `i` (the same as `i = i + 1`). If you forget that line, the condition never becomes false and the program enters an **infinite loop**.

## for: when you know how many times to repeat

`for` puts the three parts of a counted loop on a single line: **start**, **condition** and **step**.

```c
int i;
for (i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
    printf("%d ", i);
}
```

It shows `0 1 2 3 4 `. The order of execution is: start (once), test the condition, run the block, run the step, test again, and so on. Counting from 0 and using `<` is the standard in C, because arrays (lesson 7) start at position 0.

## do-while: runs at least once

```c
do {
    ...
} while (condicao);
```

The condition is tested at the end; useful when the block needs to run before the first test.

## Accumulators

A very common pattern is the **accumulator**: a variable that starts with a neutral value and is updated on every pass.

```c
int soma = 0, i, x;
for (i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    scanf("%d", &x);
    soma += x;     /* the same as soma = soma + x */
}
```

For the **sum**, the neutral value is 0. For the **product**, it is 1.

## Largest and smallest

To find the largest value, a common trap is to start with `maior = 0`. If all the numbers are negative (for example -5, -1, -9), the result would be 0, which is not even in the list! The correct solution is to **start with the first value read**:

```c
if (i == 0 || x > maior) {
    maior = x;
}
```

On the first pass (`i == 0`) the value is always accepted; on the following ones, only if it is larger.

## break and continue

- `break` leaves the loop immediately.
- `continue` skips to the next pass.

Use them sparingly: a loop with a clear condition is easier to understand.

## Special case: no data

Always think about the **empty** case. If the quantity is zero, there is no largest or smallest, and the program must handle this with an `if` before the loop (lesson 4).

c-06.theory:
# Functions: divide and conquer

Your programs are growing. Functions let you give a **name** to a piece of code, reuse it and test it separately. You have been using functions since lesson 1: `main`, `printf` and `scanf`.

```c
int quadrado(int x) {
    return x * x;
}

int main(void) {
    printf("%d\n", quadrado(7));   /* 49 */
    return 0;
}
```

- `int` before the name is the **return type**; use `void` if the function returns nothing.
- `(int x)` is the **parameter** list.
- `return` ends the function and returns the value.

In C, a function must be known **before** it is called. That is why you write the functions above `main` (or declare the prototype `int quadrado(int x);` at the top).

## Functions that answer yes or no

A function can return `1` (true) or `0` (false), like the comparisons in lesson 4. A prime number is divisible only by 1 and by itself:

```c
int ehPrimo(int n) {
    int d;
    if (n < 2) return 0;
    for (d = 2; d * d <= n; d++) {
        if (n % d == 0) return 0;
    }
    return 1;
}
```

Why `d * d <= n`? If `n` has a divisor greater than the square root, it pairs with another one **smaller** than the root, which would already have been found. This reduces the work from `n` to about the square root of `n` steps.

## Pass by value

In C, parameters receive **copies** of the values. This function does not work:

```c
void trocarErrado(int a, int b) {
    int t = a;
    a = b;
    b = t;     /* swaps only the copies! */
}
```

## Pointers: the address of a variable

Remember the `&` in `scanf`? It gives the **address** of a variable. A **pointer** is a variable that stores an address:

```c
int x = 10;
int *p = &x;    /* p points to x */
*p = 25;        /* changes x through the pointer */
printf("%d\n", x);   /* 25 */
```

- `int *p` declares a pointer to `int`.
- `&x` means "the address of x".
- `*p` means "the value at the address stored in p".

With pointers, a function can change the variables of its caller:

```c
void trocar(int *a, int *b) {
    int t = *a;
    *a = *b;
    *b = t;
}

int main(void) {
    int x = 3, y = 8;
    trocar(&x, &y);
    printf("%d %d\n", x, y);   /* 8 3 */
    return 0;
}
```

That is exactly what `scanf` does: you pass `&idade` and it writes to the address it received.

## Common mistakes

- Calling `trocar(x, y)` without `&`: the function would receive numbers, not addresses.
- Using a pointer without first pointing it somewhere (`int *p; *p = 5;` is a serious error).
- Forgetting the `return` in a function that returns `int`.

## Output separated by spaces

To show numbers separated by a space without a leftover space at the start, use a counter: write the space **before** each number, except the first.

c-07.theory:
# Arrays

So far, each value had its own variable. In lesson 5 you summed `n` numbers, but you could not store all of them to use later. An **array** stores several values of the same type, side by side in memory:

```c
int notas[5];          /* 5 integers: notas[0] .. notas[4] */
int primos[4] = {2, 3, 5, 7};
```

- The size is **fixed** and set in the declaration.
- Indexes go from `0` to `size - 1`.
- C does **not check** the bounds: accessing `notas[5]` reads or corrupts the memory of something else. This is one of the most dangerous bugs in the language. (To help, EasyCreator checks and shows an "out of bounds" error; a real compiler warns about nothing.)

Walking through an array is a perfect match for `for`:

```c
int i, soma = 0;
for (i = 0; i < 4; i++) {
    soma += primos[i];
}
```

There are also two-dimensional arrays, like a table: `int m[3][4];` and `m[linha][coluna]`.

## Arrays and pointers

The name of an array, used in an expression, becomes the **address of the first element**. So `primos` and `&primos[0]` are the same thing, and `*(primos + 2)` is the same as `primos[2]`. When you pass an array to a function, you pass that address, so the function **changes the original array** (unlike the pass by value of lesson 6):

```c
void zerar(int v[], int n) {
    int i;
    for (i = 0; i < n; i++) v[i] = 0;
}
```

Since the function does not know the size of the array, the size goes as a separate parameter.

## Strings: arrays of char

C has no "text" type. A **string** is an array of `char` ended by the special character `'\0'` (the null terminator):

```c
char nome[10] = "Ana";
/* nome[0]='A', nome[1]='n', nome[2]='a', nome[3]='\0' */
```

Always reserve **one extra space** for the `'\0'`. It is what tells `printf("%s")` where the text ends.

To read a word (without spaces), use `%s` **without** the `&`: the array name is already an address.

```c
char palavra[101];
scanf("%s", palavra);
```

## The string.h library

With `#include <string.h>` you get ready-made functions:

- `strlen(s)`: number of characters (not counting the `'\0'`)
- `strcmp(a, b)`: returns `0` if the strings are equal
- `strcpy(destino, origem)`: copies a string

Careful: `if (a == b)` with strings compares **addresses**, not the content. To compare texts always use `strcmp(a, b) == 0`. Likewise, you cannot write `destino = origem;`: use `strcpy`.

## Building a string by hand

To reverse a word of length `n`, position `i` of the result receives position `n - 1 - i` of the original. At the end, do not forget to put the `'\0'` at position `n`; without it, `printf` would keep reading memory until it happened to find a zero.

```c
for (i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    inv[i] = s[n - 1 - i];
}
inv[n] = '\0';
```

A **palindrome** is a word that is the same backwards, like "radar" and "ovo".

c-08.theory:
# Structs

Imagine storing the name, two grades and the average of 100 students. With what we have seen so far, that would be four separate arrays (`nomes`, `notas1`, `notas2`, `medias`) that you have to keep in sync by hand. A **struct** groups related data into a single new type:

```c
struct Ponto {
    int x;
    int y;
};

struct Ponto p;
p.x = 3;
p.y = 4;
```

Each item (`x`, `y`) is a **field**, accessed with the dot: `p.x`.

## typedef: giving it a short name

Writing `struct Ponto` every time is tiresome. With `typedef`, the type gets a direct name:

```c
typedef struct {
    char nome[50];
    int nota1;
    int nota2;
    double media;
} Aluno;

Aluno a;
```

Now `Aluno` is a type like `int`. A struct can contain arrays (like `nome`), numbers and even other structs.

## Arrays of structs

The power shows up when we combine them with the arrays of lesson 7:

```c
Aluno turma[100];
scanf("%s", turma[0].nome);       /* char array: no & */
scanf("%d", &turma[0].nota1);     /* int: with & */
```

Read the expression from left to right: `turma[0]` is an `Aluno`; `.nota1` is the field; `&` takes the address of that field.

## Structs and functions: copy or pointer?

Just like an `int` (lesson 6), a struct passed to a function is **copied**. If the function needs to **change** the struct, pass a pointer. To access a field through a pointer, C has the arrow operator `->`:

```c
void calcularMedia(Aluno *a) {
    a->media = (a->nota1 + a->nota2) / 2.0;
}

calcularMedia(&turma[i]);
```

`a->media` is just a more readable way to write `(*a).media`. The parentheses are mandatory in the second form, because the dot has higher precedence than the `*`.

Passing a pointer has another advantage: a large struct does not need to be copied entirely on every call; only the address (a few bytes) travels.

## Finding the "best"

To find the student with the highest average, store the **index** of the best one, not just the value: that way you still have access to the name.

```c
int melhor = 0;
for (i = 1; i < n; i++) {
    if (turma[i].media > turma[melhor].media) melhor = i;
}
```

With `>` (and not `>=`), in case of a tie the **first** one found stays. Small details like this define the program's behavior; read the specification carefully.

## Common mistakes

- Forgetting the `;` after the brace that closes the struct.
- Using `.` on a pointer (`a.media` when `a` is `Aluno *`): use `->`.
- Putting `&` before a `char nome[50]` field in `scanf("%s")`.
- Comparing structs with `==`: C does not allow it; compare field by field.

Structs are the basis of everything that comes next: lists, trees and the "objects" of languages like C++ and C# were born from this idea.

c-10.theory:
# Sorting, binary search and complexity

You already master arrays (lesson 7) and functions (lesson 6), and in lesson 9 you saw that two correct algorithms can have very different costs. Now we will measure that cost rigorously and apply the idea to two fundamental problems: **sorting** and **searching**.

## Complexity: Big O notation

We do not measure algorithms in seconds (that depends on the machine), but in **how many steps** they take as a function of the input size `n`. The **O(f(n))** notation describes how that number grows in the worst case, ignoring constants:

- **O(1)**: constant. Accessing `v[i]`.
- **O(log n)**: logarithmic. At each step, the problem is cut in half. For n = 1 million, about 20 steps.
- **O(n)**: linear. Walking through the array once.
- **O(n log n)**: the best comparison-based sorting algorithms (merge sort, heap sort, quick sort in the average case).
- **O(n²)**: quadratic. Two nested loops over the array. For n = 1 million, 10¹² steps: infeasible.

The difference between classes dominates any constant as `n` grows. That is why choosing the right algorithm matters more than micro-optimizing the code.

## Insertion sort

It is the method of someone organizing cards in their hand: take each element and **insert it** at the right position among those already sorted on the left, shifting the larger ones one place to the right.

```c
void ordenar(int v[], int n) {
    int i, j, chave;
    for (i = 1; i < n; i++) {
        chave = v[i];
        j = i - 1;
        while (j >= 0 && v[j] > chave) {
            v[j + 1] = v[j];
            j--;
        }
        v[j + 1] = chave;
    }
}
```

Note the short-circuit from lesson 4: if `j >= 0` is false, `v[j]` is not even accessed (avoiding `v[-1]`).

Analysis: in the **worst case** (array in descending order), element `i` is compared with all `i` previous ones: 1 + 2 + ... + (n-1) = n(n-1)/2, that is, **O(n²)**. In the **best case** (already sorted), each `while` stops at the first comparison: **O(n)**. It sorts **in place** (extra memory O(1)) and is **stable** (equal elements keep their order). For small arrays it is excellent; for large ones, prefer O(n log n).

## Linear search versus binary search

Looking for a value one by one costs **O(n)**. But if the array is **sorted**, you can do much better: look at the **middle** element. If it is the one you want, you found it. If it is smaller, the value can only be on the right; if it is larger, on the left. Each comparison **discards half** of what remains.

```c
int buscaBinaria(int v[], int n, int x) {
    int ini = 0, fim = n - 1, meio;
    while (ini <= fim) {
        meio = (ini + fim) / 2;
        if (v[meio] == x) return meio;
        if (v[meio] < x) ini = meio + 1;
        else fim = meio - 1;
    }
    return -1;   /* not found */
}
```

After k steps, n / 2^k elements remain; the search ends when 1 is left, that is, k = log₂ n. Therefore, **O(log n)**. For 1 billion elements: at most 30 passes of the loop (each looks at a single element), against up to 1 billion elements looked at in the linear search.

## The total cost

Sorting costs O(n²) with insertion sort (or O(n log n) with a better algorithm) and each search costs O(log n). If you are going to do **many** searches on the same array, sorting once pays off. For a single search, the linear O(n) one would be cheaper than sorting. This kind of cost-benefit reasoning is the heart of algorithm analysis.

## Common mistakes

- Doing binary search on an **unsorted** array: the result is unpredictable.
- Using `ini < fim` instead of `ini <= fim`: the last candidate is not tested.
- Forgetting the `+ 1` / `- 1` when moving the limits: infinite loop.

## Constants with #define

For the maximum size of the array, it is common to use `#define MAX 100` at the top and declare `int v[MAX];`. That way the number appears in one place only.

c-13.theory:
# Parsing text character by character

Compilers, calculators, spreadsheets and configuration file readers all do the same thing: they receive a **text** and figure out what it means. This work is called **lexical analysis** (splitting the text into pieces, the *tokens*) and **syntax analysis** (understanding how the pieces combine). In this lesson you will write a small calculator like that.

## Reading a whole line: fgets

`scanf("%s")` stops at the first space. To read the whole line, use `fgets`:

```c
char linha[201];
fgets(linha, 201, stdin);
```

It reads up to 200 characters (leaving room for the `'\0'`) or until the end of the line, and **keeps the `'\n'`** in the text. If needed, remove it:

```c
int n = strlen(linha);
if (n > 0 && linha[n - 1] == '\n') linha[n - 1] = '\0';
```

If there is nothing to read, `fgets` returns `NULL`.

## Classifying characters: <ctype.h>

- `isdigit(c)`: true if `c` is a digit `'0'` to `'9'`;
- `isspace(c)`: space, tab or line break;
- `isalpha(c)`: a letter.

## From text to number

The character `'7'` is not the number 7: it is code 55 in the ASCII table. Since the digits are consecutive in the table, `c - '0'` gives the digit's value. To build a number with several digits, multiply what you have by 10 and add the new digit:

```c
int valor = 0;
while (isdigit(*p)) {
    valor = valor * 10 + (*p - '0');   /* "472": 4, 47, 472 */
    p++;
}
```

That is exactly what `atoi` does inside.

## The parser loop

A pointer `p` goes through the line. On each turn, we look at the current character and decide:

```c
char *p = linha;
while (*p != '\0') {
    if (isspace(*p)) {
        p++;                    /* skip spaces */
    } else if (isdigit(*p)) {
        /* read the whole number and add it with the current sign */
    } else if (*p == '+' || *p == '-') {
        /* keep the sign for the next number */
        p++;
    } else {
        /* invalid character: error */
    }
}
```

Notice that reading a number **advances** the pointer by several characters at once, while the other cases advance just one. The position of the current character is `p - linha` (pointer arithmetic, lesson 11).

## Useful error messages

A good parser says **where** the problem is. Compilers show line and column; here, the column is `(p - linha) + 1` (counting from 1, the way people count).

## Common mistakes

- Adding `*p` instead of `*p - '0'`: `'7'` is worth 55.
- Forgetting to advance `p` in some case: infinite loop.
- Testing only `' '` as a space and tripping over the `'\n'` that `fgets` left at the end.

c-14.theory:
# Dynamic memory

Until now every array had a fixed size written in the code (`int v[100]`). But what if you don't know how much data will come? Reserving a million positions "just in case" wastes memory, and it still might not be enough. The solution is to ask for memory **while the program runs**.

## Stack and heap

Local variables live on the **stack**: they are created when the function starts and disappear when it ends, automatically. Dynamic memory comes from another region, the **heap**: you ask for it, use it for as long as you want and **give it back** when you no longer need it. Nobody gives it back for you.

## malloc: asking for memory

```c
#include <stdlib.h>

int *v = malloc(10 * sizeof(int));   /* room for 10 ints */
if (v == NULL) {
    /* there was no memory: handle the error */
}
v[0] = 42;                           /* use it like a normal array */
```

`malloc` receives the number of **bytes** and returns a pointer to the start of the block, or `NULL` if it fails. That is why we use `sizeof(int)`: the size of an `int` changes from machine to machine. The initial content is garbage; `calloc(10, sizeof(int))` does the same, but zeroes everything.

## free: giving it back

```c
free(v);
v = NULL;   /* good practice: avoids using the block after it was freed */
```

Forgetting `free` causes a **memory leak**: programs that run for a long time, like servers, consume more and more until they crash. Using the block after `free` (*use after free*) or freeing it twice are serious mistakes, classic sources of security flaws.

## realloc: changing the size

```c
int *novo = realloc(v, 20 * sizeof(int));
if (novo == NULL) { free(v); /* error */ }
v = novo;
```

`realloc` grows (or shrinks) the block, **keeping the content**. If it doesn't fit in the same place, it copies everything to a new block and frees the old one. That is why we store the result in a helper variable: if it fails, the old block is still valid.

## The dynamic array (which grows by itself)

It is the structure behind Python's `list`, Java's `ArrayList` and C#'s `List<T>`. We keep three things: the pointer, how many elements there are (`n`) and how many fit (`capacidade`). When it is full, we **double** the capacity:

```c
if (n == capacidade) {
    capacidade *= 2;
    v = realloc(v, capacidade * sizeof(int));
}
v[n] = x;
n++;
```

Why double instead of adding 1? Each `realloc` may copy the whole array. Growing one by one, inserting `n` elements would cost 1 + 2 + ... + n = **O(n²)** copies. Doubling, the copies add up to at most 2n: each insertion costs **O(1) amortized**, on average.

## Reading until it ends

`scanf` returns how many values it managed to read. With `while (scanf("%d", &x) == 1 && x != 0)` the loop stops at 0 **or** at the end of the input.

c-15.theory:
# Linked lists

An array keeps its elements side by side in memory. That makes access `v[i]` instant, but inserting in the middle is expensive: you have to push all the following ones one slot forward. A **linked list** makes the opposite trade: each element lives in a separate block (a **node**) and stores the **address of the next one**.

```
cabeca -> [3 | *] -> [5 | *] -> [8 | NULL]
```

## The node: a struct that points to itself

```c
typedef struct No {
    int valor;
    struct No *prox;   /* address of the next node (or NULL in the last one) */
} No;
```

A struct cannot **contain** itself (it would have infinite size), but it can contain a **pointer** to another one of the same type. That is why we need the name `struct No` inside its own definition.

The whole list is represented by a single pointer, the **head**: `No *cabeca = NULL;` is the empty list.

## Traversing

```c
No *p;
for (p = cabeca; p != NULL; p = p->prox) {
    printf("%d ", p->valor);
}
```

There is no index: to reach the fifth element you have to go through the first four. Access by position costs **O(n)**.

## Inserting at the beginning: O(1)

```c
No *novo = malloc(sizeof(No));
novo->valor = 42;
novo->prox = cabeca;   /* the new one points to the old first */
cabeca = novo;         /* and becomes the head */
```

## Inserting in order

To keep the list sorted, walk to the last node whose value is **less than or equal to** the new one and fit the new node right after it. Watch out for the special case: if the list is empty or the new value is smaller than the head, it becomes the new head. That is why the function **returns the head** (which may have changed): `cabeca = inserirOrdenado(cabeca, 5);`.

## Removing

To remove a node, the **previous** one must start pointing to the next. That is why we walk with two pointers, `ant` and `atual`:

```c
while (atual != NULL && atual->valor != valor) {
    ant = atual;
    atual = atual->prox;
}
if (atual != NULL) {
    if (ant == NULL) cabeca = atual->prox;   /* it was the head */
    else ant->prox = atual->prox;
    free(atual);
}
```

Experienced programmers sometimes use a pointer to a pointer (`No **`) to remove the special case of the head; it is a good exercise after this lesson.

## Freeing the whole list

Each node came from a `malloc`, so each one needs a `free`. Save the next one **before** freeing the current one:

```c
while (cabeca != NULL) {
    No *prox = cabeca->prox;
    free(cabeca);
    cabeca = prox;
}
```

## Array or list?

| Operation | Array | Linked list |
|---|---|---|
| access the i-th | O(1) | O(n) |
| insert/remove at the start | O(n) | O(1) |
| insert after a known node | O(n) | O(1) |

Linked lists show up inside operating systems, memory allocators and hash tables (lesson 19).

c-18.theory:
# Divide and conquer: merge sort

In lesson 10, insertion sort cost O(n²). For a million numbers, that is about 10¹² comparisons: hours. **Merge sort** does the same job in **O(n log n)**: some 20 million steps, a fraction of a second. It is the classic example of the **divide and conquer** technique:

1. **Divide**: cut the array in half.
2. **Conquer**: sort each half, recursively (a half of size 1 is already sorted: that is the base case).
3. **Combine**: **merge** the two sorted halves into one.

## Merging

With two halves already sorted, just look at the first of each one and copy the **smaller** into a helper array, repeating until one runs out; then copy whatever is left of the other:

```c
/* halves v[ini..meio-1] and v[meio..fim-1] already sorted */
i = ini; j = meio; k = ini;
while (i < meio && j < fim) {
    if (v[i] <= v[j]) tmp[k++] = v[i++];
    else              tmp[k++] = v[j++];
}
while (i < meio) tmp[k++] = v[i++];
while (j < fim)  tmp[k++] = v[j++];
for (k = ini; k < fim; k++) v[k] = tmp[k];
```

`tmp[k++] = v[i++]` uses the value of `k` and of `i` **and then** adds 1 to each of them. Using `<=` (and not `<`) keeps the order of equal elements: merge sort is **stable**.

## Why O(n log n)?

Each level of the recursion splits the pieces in half, so there are **log₂ n** levels. On each level, the merges together go through the **n** elements once. Total: n · log₂ n. The price is the extra O(n) memory of the `tmp` array, which we request with `malloc` (lesson 14).

## Inversions

An **inversion** is a pair of positions `i < j` with `v[i] > v[j]`: two elements "out of order". The array `2 4 1 3 5` has 3 inversions: (2,1), (4,1) and (4,3). A sorted array has 0; one in decreasing order has the maximum, n(n−1)/2. The number of inversions measures **how unsorted** an array is: it is exactly the number of swaps bubble sort would make. Recommendation systems use this measure to compare two rankings (how much my taste resembles yours).

Counting by testing every pair is O(n²). But merge sort counts them for free: during the merge, when an element of the **right half** (`v[j]`) is copied before the ones still left in the left half, it is smaller than **all** of them, and each one forms an inversion. There are `meio - i` elements left on the left:

```c
else {
    tmp[k++] = v[j++];
    inversoes += meio - i;
}
```

The total is the sum of the inversions on the left, on the right and the "crossed" ones found in the merge. Use `long`: with n = 100,000, they can exceed 4 billion.

## Quick sort and the library

Another famous algorithm is **quick sort**: it picks a pivot, separates smaller and larger values and sorts each side. On average it is O(n log n) and very fast in practice, but in the worst case it drops to O(n²). Real libraries combine ideas: C++'s `sort` uses *introsort*, and Python and Java use *Timsort*, a relative of merge sort.

